df['week'] = ((df['date'] - df['date'].dt.to_period('Y').dt.start_time).dt.days // 7) + 1

df['week'] =

We are creating a new column called week in the dataframe.

df['date']

This is your date column, e.g.

2023-01-01
2023-01-02
2023-01-08

.dt

This tells pandas:

“I want to use date-time operations on this column.”

Without .dt, pandas cannot extract year, month, etc.

.to_period('Y')

This converts each date into a year period.

Example:

2023-05-10  →  2023
2024-11-03  →  2024


So now pandas knows:

“This date belongs to this year.”

.dt.start_time

This gives the first moment of that year.

Example:

2023 → 2023-01-01 00:00:00
2024 → 2024-01-01 00:00:00


So for every date, we now have January 1st of that same year.

df['date'] - Jan 1

This subtracts:

actual_date − Jan_1_of_that_year


Result = time difference (called Timedelta).

Examples:

2023-01-01 − 2023-01-01 = 0 days
2023-01-07 − 2023-01-01 = 6 days
2023-01-08 − 2023-01-01 = 7 days

.dt.days

This converts the time difference into number of days.

Example:

0 days
6 days
7 days


Now we have:

“How many days have passed since Jan 1?”

// 7

This is integer division.

It groups days into blocks of 7 days.

Examples:

0 // 7 = 0
6 // 7 = 0
7 // 7 = 1
13 // 7 = 1
14 // 7 = 2


So:

Days 0–6 → group 0

Days 7–13 → group 1

Days 14–20 → group 2

Each group represents a week.

+ 1

Because week numbers should start from 1, not 0.

Final result:

Days 0–6   → Week 1
Days 7–13  → Week 2
Days 14–20 → Week 3


✅ This gives simple, business-friendly week numbers starting from Jan 1.

